  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)=x(x+3)(x-2).$ On which of the following intervals does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that the equation $f(x)=-5$ has a solution?\n\n- $[-5,-3]$\n- $[-3,-2]$\n- $[-2,0]$",
  "worked_solution": "Since $f(x)$ is a polynomial, it's continuous over the real numbers. Thus, we can apply the intermediate value theorem to any of the given intervals.\n\nLet's make a table with the values of $f(x)$ at the endpoints.\n\n| $x$ | $-5$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-70$ | $0$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\n- On the interval $[-5,-3],$ we have $f(-5)=-70$ and $f(-3)=0.$ Since $-5\\in [-70,0],$ the IVT does guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-5$ on this interval.\n\n- On the interval $[-3,-2],$ we have $f(-3)=0$ and $f(-2)=8.$ Since $-5\\notin [0,8],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-5$ on this interval.\n\n- On the interval $[-2,0],$ we have $f(-2)=8$ and $f(0)=0.$ Since $-5\\notin [0,8],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-5$ on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
